*Message recovery.* Broker-outage recovery remains designed, not tested on AWS: a committed outbox row survives a broker outage, because the relay retries publication to SNS up to ten times (`OutboxRelayService.MAX_ATTEMPTS`). SQS moves a message that fails five receives to its DLQ (AD-04, §3.2.3). Redelivery after a consumer commit is safe, because the consumer records each event in `sys_consumed_message`. Notifications can therefore arrive later, and exhausted relay retries or dead-letter messages need an operator (#src("backend/docs/adr/0007-cross-service-events-sns-sqs.md")[integration decision], #src("backend/blocbuy-infrastructure/src/main/java/com/blocbuy/notification/messaging/SqsNotificationEventConsumer.java")[queue consumer], #src("infra/swe5001/modules/messaging/main.tf")[queue and alarm configuration]).

*V7 poison-message drill.* On 14 November 2026 on AWS, V7 R4 passed five production receives and isolation: the valid transport control produced exactly *1/1/1* consumed marker, notification and intended-recipient notification; poison effects were *0/0* markers/notifications. Only the identified poison was deleted, and quiet queues, a real ALARM transition and its reset were verified. Injection → ALARM was *233.129957 s* and sampled observed DLQ → ALARM *158.232215 s*, both below the *300 s* limits; the latter is not exact DLQ enqueue latency. The owner's matched email receipt was *10:12 SGT*, minute-only, establishing no exact delivery timestamp or latency. Sources: #published-evidence("20261114-v7-r4-live/README.md")[V7 R4 record] and #published-evidence("20261114-v7-r4-live/acceptance.json")[acceptance].

That pass used temporary *10 s application consumer visibility*: the application image, resources, workers and queue/scaling policies were unchanged, and source-queue visibility remained *60 s*. The original *60 s consumer visibility* was restored and verified at *10:31:54 SGT* on 14 November. Its timing remains unproven. R1 took *515.129 s* from injection to alarm and failed; R2/R3 failed during setup before message publication.

*Identity outage.* The first AWS attempt, R1 on 13 November 2026, failed: START returned *HTTP 200 instead of the expected 500*, and healthy recovery took *217.828 s*, exceeding the *180 s* gate. Service counts reached zero before the old application shut down, so the probe did not establish application unavailability (#published-evidence("20261113-aws-validation/identity-r1.md")[R1 record]). The 15 November R11 check in @tbl-followup-15nov waited for actual application shutdown and demonstrated initiation failure followed by recovery. It covers payment initiation, not card confirmation, signed-webhook failure/redelivery or replay; those broader AWS scenarios remain unverified.

*Sources.* The records in @tbl-followup-15nov are retained in repository snapshot `71b06b5` and cited as filenames relative to `docs/swe5001/evidence/20261115-acceptance-followup/`; this snapshot is local, not published online. Other validation hyperlinks use the published evidence branch and are mutable. Source-code citations retain the tested application revision.

